	'register' => 'The whole site is formal dumneavoastră, in every string and every slot — body copy, headings, eyebrows, ALL-CAPS bands, buttons, form labels, placeholders, alt and aria text, the <title>, the meta description, og: tags and JSON-LD. Register is a property of the PAGE, not of the segment: a tu-form in a meta description contradicts the dumneavoastră body it ships with. The verb is second-person plural and any predicative adjective agreeing with the reader is plural: „Aveți nevoie de asistență?”, „Ne puteți suna”, „Nu știți ce vă spun datele?”, „Sunteți pregătiți să vă modernizați infrastructura?” — never „poți”, never „Nu sunteți sigur”. The reader-facing possessive is „dumneavoastră”, always in full, never „dvs.”. No tu form (tău, ta, tine, poți, vrei, vezi, începi) appears anywhere, at any length. Romanian business prose tolerates longer subordinate-clause sentences than English marketing copy — do not chop them into fragments and do not pad them; an impersonal construction („se poate configura”) often reads more professional than a second-person imperative in descriptive prose. BUTTONS AND LINK LABELS keep the verb of the English and use the polite plural imperative, standard Romanian web UI: „Browse catalog” = „Consultați catalogul”, „Request a demo” = „Solicitați o demonstrație”, „Download” = „Descărcați”, „Contact us” = „Contactați-ne”, „Search” = „Căutați”. Never flatten a verb label into a bare noun („Catalog”, „Descărcare” are wrong for those), and a control label carries no pronoun or possessive („Protejați-vă investiția” is wrong; „Protecția investiției” is right). Everything that is not a control label addresses the reader only as dumneavoastră.',
